Q: Scroll is janky, while items are not rebuilding A: <p>Flutter web apps can have janky scrolling on Safari/iOS because its rendering engine doesn’t always utilize GPU acceleration properly. Here’s how to fix it.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;!-- CSS: Safari Scroll &amp; Canvas Optimization --&gt;
&lt;style&gt;
html, body, flt-scene-host, canvas {
  transform: translate3d(0,0,0);
  -webkit-transform: translate3d(0,0,0);
  will-change: transform;
  backface-visibility: hidden;
  -webkit-backface-visibility: hidden;
  -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
}
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;!-- JS: Scroll Throttling &amp; Safari Detection --&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
(function() {
  const isSafari = /^((?!chrome|android).)*safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent) ||
                   /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);
  if (!isSafari) return;

  // Throttle scroll events for better performance
  let last = 0;
  const throttle = (fn) =&gt; (e) =&gt; {
    const now = performance.now();
    if (now - last &gt; 16) { last = now; fn(e); last = now; }
  };

  const originalAddEventListener = EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener;
  EventTarget.prototype.addEventListener = function(type, listener, options) {
    if (type === 'scroll') {
      return originalAddEventListener.call(this, type, throttle(listener), { ...options, passive: true });
    }
    return originalAddEventListener.call(this, type, listener, options);
  };
})();
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>If you want <strong>full Safari-specific optimization</strong>, including dynamic Flutter elements and canvas rendering tweaks, you can extend your <code>web/index.html</code> like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;script&gt;
(function() {
  const isSafari = /^((?!chrome|android).)*safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent) ||
                   /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);
  if (!isSafari) return;

  const optimizeElement = (el) =&gt; {
    if (!el) return;
    el.style.transform = 'translate3d(0,0,0)';
    el.style.webkitTransform = 'translate3d(0,0,0)';
    el.style.willChange = 'transform';
    el.style.backfaceVisibility = 'hidden';
    el.style.webkitBackfaceVisibility = 'hidden';
    el.style.isolation = 'isolate';
    if (el.tagName === 'CANVAS') {
      el.style.contain = 'strict';
      el.style.imageRendering = '-webkit-optimize-contrast';
    }
  };

  // Optimize Flutter elements
  const optimizeFlutterElements = () =&gt; {
    document.querySelectorAll('canvas, flt-scene-host, flt-glass-pane, flt-scene, flt-view-semantics')
            .forEach(optimizeElement);
  };

  // Observe dynamic elements
  new MutationObserver(optimizeFlutterElements)
    .observe(document.body, { childList: true, subtree: true });

  window.addEventListener('load', () =&gt; {
    optimizeFlutterElements();
    setInterval(optimizeFlutterElements, 1000);

    // Canvas context optimization
    document.querySelectorAll('canvas').forEach(canvas =&gt; {
      try {
        const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d', { alpha:false, desynchronized:true, willReadFrequently:false });
        if (ctx) { ctx.imageSmoothingEnabled = false; ctx.webkitImageSmoothingEnabled = false; }
      } catch {}
    });
  });
})();
&lt;/script&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Why This Works</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><p><code>translate3d(0,0,0)</code> → Forces GPU acceleration (composite layer).</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>will-change: transform</code> → Browser knows element will change.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>backface-visibility: hidden</code> → Optimizes 3D transforms.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>contain: layout style paint</code> → Limits layout/paint cost.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>-webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch</code> → Enables momentum scrolling.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Scroll throttling + passive listeners → Reduces event overhead.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Canvas context tweaks → Reduces paint work, disables alpha channel for faster rendering.</p>
</li>
</ol>

Q: How to use reduce() with gt to compute multiple summary statistics rows? A: <p>Using <code>reduce</code> from package {purrr} (but base R <code>Map</code> would do as well):</p>
<pre><code>library(purrr)

years &lt;- c(2020, 2025) |&gt; as.character()

years |&gt; 
  reduce(.f = \(my_gt, year){ 
    my_gt |&gt; 
      grand_summary_rows(
        columns = ends_with(year),
        fns = sprintf(&quot;Total ~ sum(%s * %s) / sum(%s)&quot;,
                      paste0(&quot;p&quot;, year),
                      paste0(&quot;w&quot;, year),
                      paste0(&quot;w&quot;, year)
        ) |&gt; as.formula()
      )
  },
  .init = df |&gt; gt(rowname_col = &quot;group&quot;)
  ) |&gt; 
  cols_hide(starts_with(&quot;w&quot;))

</code></pre>
<p>returns:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/0vQF9ZCY.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/0vQF9ZCY.png" alt="using reduce on gt" /></a></p>
<p>Note the use of <code>.init</code> to give <code>reduce</code> an initial <code>gt</code> to successively add <code>grand_summary_rows</code>.</p>
